  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nDry Through Friday:\n\nLow level cold air advection in the wake of the upper level trough \nshifting east across the northern Great Lakes on GOES water vapor \nimagery loops will steepen low level lapse rates through Wednesday \nresulting in breezy northwest winds, strongest west of the \nMississippi River Valley, and low (10-25%) precipitation chances, \nmainly along and north of Interstate 94 locally in central Wisconsin \nwhere residual low level moisture coincides with strongest CAA. \n\nMinimal progression in the synoptic pattern through the end of the \nwork week perpetuates apparent temperatures in the mid to upper 70s \nthrough Thursday, breaching the low 80s Friday and Saturday before \nreturn flow ramps up heat and storm chances through the weekend into \nearly next week.\n\nWeekend Precipitation & Storm Chances:\n\nAccording to longer range global ensembles, passing perturbations\ninitially Friday contend and attempt to flatten the residual mid\nlevel ridge over the Upper Midwest, weakening mid level vorticity\nadvection as it nears the Upper Mississippi River Valley. While\nboth the GEFS and EPS have trended earlier for initial precipitation\nchances Friday night into Saturday morning, the GEFS solution \nremains more boisterous and slightly earlier than the EPS due to\na more southern, stronger solution to mid level vorticity \nadvection through the Northern Plains. A wide spread in synoptic\nsolutions leaves little confidence in storm and severe threat \nlocally, evident in inter/intraensemble SBCAPE/MUCAPE spread of\n0 to 4500 J/kg locally in both the EPS and GEFS.",
